  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 211,
      "text": "MEXICO\nAuthorities’ Views\n34. The authorities consider that the country’s external buffers served them well during\nthe pandemic and in the recent tightening period. The flexible exchange rate helped support the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 211,
      "end_char": 412,
      "text": "recovery by stimulating manufacturing. Portfolio outflows have proved manageable in the context of\nsubstantial remittances and recently resurgent FDI, while the level of foreign participation in local\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 412,
      "end_char": 639,
      "text": "currency sovereign bond market, now back to earlier levels, lowers risks to future external shocks.\nC. Maintaining a Robust Financial System\n35. The financial system has emerged from the pandemic in good health. Non-performing\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 639,
      "end_char": 836,
      "text": "loans (NPLs) rose modestly from low levels during the pandemic and began declining last year.\nCapital adequacy ratios are high and rising, and liquidity ratios are comfortable. With these buffers,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 836,
      "end_char": 1032,
      "text": "the banking system demonstrated strong resilience to severe macrofinancial shocks in the solvency\nand liquidity stress tests conducted under the 2022 FSAP (although some smaller banks may require\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 1032,
      "end_char": 1238,
      "text": "additional buffers to handle such stresses). Overall, systemic vulnerabilities and liquidity risks in the\nfinancial system appear broadly contained, given high capital buffers, low private sector leverage,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1238,
      "end_char": 1470,
      "text": "and no sign of stretched asset prices.\n36. Bank loans and the financial health of non-financial corporations should be monitored\nin the context of the recent rapid rise of interest rates. The increase itself should, all else equal,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1470,
      "end_char": 1659,
      "text": "increase banks’ net interest margin, especially for larger institutions. On the other hand, with\nMexican banks having increased their holdings of domestic government bonds, they could face\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1659,
      "end_char": 1865,
      "text": "mark to market losses on their holdings (although the impact is not expected to be large since their\nholdings are of short duration). A large fraction of non-financial corporate credit is floating rate, so\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1865,
      "end_char": 2067,
      "text": "higher interest payments could strain liquidity for some corporates and possibly increase default\nrates. Offsetting the pressures from higher interest rates, Mexican corporates retain relatively strong\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 2067,
      "end_char": 2277,
      "text": "buffers and FSAP stress tests imply that heightened default risks under the adverse scenario from\ncurrently low levels would be manageable for the banking sector.\nNFC Average Loan Rate and 6-month Average of 5\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2277,
      "end_char": 2486,
      "text": "Total Bank Lending to Non-Financial Corporates\nYear TIIESwap\n(Percent of GDP)\n(Percent) 200\n10\n9 160\n8\n120\n7\n6 80\n5\n40\nNon-financial corporate average loan rate\n4\n6-month rolling average of 5-yr TIIE swap\n0\n3\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2486,
      "end_char": 2555,
      "text": "Sources: Bloomberg and BIS Statistics.\nINTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND 19"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 24",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 198,
      "text": "MEXICO\n37. The authorities should take steps to further enhance the resilience of the financial\nsystem. The authorities have already rolled out critical Basel reforms, including the introduction of\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 198,
      "end_char": 378,
      "text": "Total Loss Absorbing Capacity (TLAC) requirements and improved supervisory techniques and\nmethodologies. The 2022 FSAP offers several recommendations for further steps (Annex XI).\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 378,
      "end_char": 595,
      "text": "• Capacity of regulatory agencies. The autonomy and resources of regulatory government\nagencies and the legal protection of supervisors could be strengthened, to equip them to deal\nwith the evolving risk environment.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 595,
      "end_char": 767,
      "text": "• Banking supervision and regulation. The banking regulator (CNBV) should continue to\nimprove supervisory techniques, by simplifying its risk-based rating system and using\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 767,
      "end_char": 947,
      "text": "methodologies that are principles- rather than rules-based. It should also be enabled to\nsupervise effectively all financial conglomerates on a consolidated basis. Currently, such\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 947,
      "end_char": 1176,
      "text": "conglomerates can voluntarily request authorization to operate as a financial group, but some\nhave not done so. The authorities should amend the 2014 Financial Groups law to make\napplication mandatory for all such conglomerates.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1176,
      "end_char": 1357,
      "text": "• Systemic liquidity management. System-wide liquidity risks appear contained, with\ncommercial banks well-placed to provide liquidity to other financial institutions during periods\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1357,
      "end_char": 1540,
      "text": "of stress. Moreover, the authorities have demonstrated the effectiveness of their toolkit to\nsupport systemic liquidity during the pandemic. But conditions bear continued monitoring,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1540,
      "end_char": 1723,
      "text": "particularly in a context of severe downside risks, and high levels of short-term wholesale\nfunding of development banks deserve further consideration, though risks are attenuated by\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1723,
      "end_char": 1942,
      "text": "their liabilities guaranteed by the sovereign. Banxico’s Emergency Liquidity Assistance\nframework could be further enhanced.\n• Safety nets. CNBV should closely monitor risks from loan concentration risks and contingent\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1942,
      "end_char": 2126,
      "text": "credit lines to non-financial corporates and apply Pillar 2 requirements as needed. Bank\nresolution and recovery could be strengthened by removing impediments to banks’ resolvability,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2126,
      "end_char": 2317,
      "text": "eliminating barriers to the effective use of the purchase and assumption and bridge bank tools,\nand expanding the resolution regime’s remit to financial holding companies. Board vacancies at\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2317,
      "end_char": 2483,
      "text": "the Deposit Insurance and Resolution Authority (IPAB) should be filled.\n• Cybersecurity. Banxico and CNBV could enhance their approach to strengthening cybersecurity\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2483,
      "end_char": 2723,
      "text": "in the financial system, by further developing their strategy and enhancing oversight, inspection,\nand investigative powers and instruments.\n• Climate risks. Financial system exposure to physical and transition risks from climate change is\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2723,
      "end_char": 2935,
      "text": "manageable but financial tail risks will worsen, particularly if investments in resilience are not\nmade and global climate policy action is delayed, resulting in higher corporate defaults and\nlower bank capital.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00016",
      "start_char": 2935,
      "end_char": 3149,
      "text": "38. The macrofinancial toolkit could benefit from additional instruments. While banking\nsystem and corporate sector risks appear contained, the mortgage market is less well regulated.\n20 INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND"
    }
  ]
}
